// A capture plan (`dev_capture::plan::CapturePlan`), the game's damage-model view (game state
// only): the player's own guns firing at the offline target, so the muzzle dressing's sprites
// (`vfx::muzzle`, `vfx_billboard.wgsl`) are on screen. The offline target scenario
// `OVERMATCH_TARGET=ahead` parks a target broadside ahead and `SPIKE_AUTO_FIRE=1` holds the
// machine guns' triggers from 20 to 50 s and the main gun's from 60 to 75 s (`command`); both run
// on their own clocks, so this plan fixes no frame step and its frames land a little differently
// run to run. The 88's flash, fireball and ground blast at 60 s were still on screen at 63 s in one
// run (MEASURED). The aim is picked through the window's centre, so the frame keeps the hidden
// window's logical size. From the checkout root, hidden and silent under the machine's build lock:
//
//   /usr/bin/lockf -k -t 10800 /tmp/overmatch-cargo.lock env OVERMATCH_TARGET=ahead SPIKE_AUTO_FIRE=1 \
//     OVERMATCH_UNATTENDED=1 OVERMATCH_WINDOW=hidden OVERMATCH_MUTE=1 \
//     OVERMATCH_CAPTURE=scripts/capture/muzzle-vfx.ron target/debug/overmatch --offline
//
// writes target/capture/muzzle-vfx-<second>.png at each frame second, then exits. Times and size
// are capture configuration (ASSUMED), not game data.
(
    frames: (
        out: "target/capture/muzzle-vfx.png",
        at: [30.0, 61.0, 63.0],
        size: (1280, 720),
        scale_factor: 1.0,
        ui: false,
        gizmos: false,
    ),
)
